  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;of chloroform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>334</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;of aniline&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>457</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>thus can be seprated of simple distillation.</p><p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;: Mixture of aniline and water seprated by simple distillation.</p><p>A suitable method for separating a mixture of aniline and chloro form would be steam distillation. Steam distillation is the process used to separate aromatic compound from a mixture because of their temperature sensitivity. Therefore, steam distillation is an ideal method for their separation</p>"
